 "prompt_sha256": "6a03fc6b3513f9c7aee92fb9a4dceb7803becc5fbe99988bad8df67c68e6d6ae",
 "answer": "The feature that allows a pgvector approximate index to keep scanning until enough rows are found is called \"iterative index scans.\" This feature enables the index to scan more of its entries until it retrieves a sufficient number of results or reaches predefined limits, such as `hnsw.max_scan_tuples` or `ivfflat.max_probes`. Iterative index scans can be configured to use either strict or relaxed ordering.\n\n- **Strict ordering** ensures that the results are returned in the exact order by distance.\n- **Relaxed ordering** allows results to be slightly out of order by distance but improves recall by scanning more of the index.\n\nHere's the relevant excerpt from the documentation:\n\n> \"With approximate indexes, queries with filtering can return less results since filtering is applied *after* the index is scanned. Starting with 0.8.0, you can enable iterative index scans, which will automatically scan more of the index until enough results are found (or it reaches `hnsw.max_scan_tuples` or `ivfflat.max_probes`).\"\n\n- **Strict Iterative Scan Example**:\n  ```sql\n  SET hnsw.iterative_scan = strict_order;\n  ```\n\n- **Relaxed Iterative Scan Example**:\n  ```sql\n  SET hnsw.iterative_scan = relaxed_order;\n  # or\n  SET ivfflat.iterative_scan = relaxed_order;\n  ```\n\nThis feature helps address situations where fewer results are returned due to limitations such as the size of the dynamic candidate list (`hnsw.ef_search`) or filtering conditions. \n\n[\"With approximate indexes, queries with filtering can return less results since filtering is applied *after* the index is scanned. Starting with 0.8.0, you can enable iterative index scans, which will automatically scan more of the index until enough results are found (or it reaches `hnsw.max_scan_tuples` or `ivfflat.max_probes`).\"](pgvector/README.md (lines 478-516))",
